{
  "schedule": {
    "frequency": "daily",
    "time": "02:30"
  },
  "retention": {
    "copies_to_keep": 14
  },
  "sources": ["/srv/data", "/srv/photos"],
  "exclusions": ["*.tmp"],
  "notifications": {
    "on_failure": ["ops@example.com"],
    "on_success": []
  }
}
```

Decisions made while structuring, each worth confirming: the schedule is split into frequency and time rather than a cron string, because 'nightly at 02:30' is what you specified and a cron expression would smuggle in weekday assumptions; notifications are modelled as lists per event, so adding a success recipient later is a data change, not a schema change; and retention is a count of copies, not days — with a nightly schedule they coincide, but if the schedule ever changes, the count is the requirement you actually stated. Nothing in the requirements mentioned compression, encryption, or a destination, so all three are absent rather than invented — a config structure should encode decisions made, and visibly lack the ones still open.

```json
{
  "symptom": "Blank screen after clicking Pay, ~5% of attempts, worse on mobile, payment sometimes completes",
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "rank": 1,
      "hypothesis": "The payment request succeeds but the success-screen render crashes on a null field that is only sometimes null (e.g. a missing confirmation code on one payment path)",
      "confirm_if": "Server logs show successful charges with no corresponding success-page render; the blank cases correlate with one payment method or issuer",
      "kill_if": "Blank-screen sessions show no completed charge and no render attempt at all"
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "hypothesis": "A race between the payment redirect and a mobile browser suspending the tab, so the return navigation never completes",
      "confirm_if": "Failures cluster on mobile browsers and on slower networks; charges complete server-side",
      "kill_if": "Reproducible on desktop with a fast wired connection at the same rate"
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "hypothesis": "A third-party script (analytics or fraud check) intermittently blocks the main thread or throws before the app bundle runs",
      "confirm_if": "Failure rate tracks the third party's outage windows; disabling it in staging removes the failures",
      "kill_if": "Failures persist with all third-party scripts removed"
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "hypothesis": "Session expiry during long form completion, so the pay request is rejected and the UI has no error state for that response",
      "confirm_if": "Failures correlate with time-on-page before clicking Pay; server returns 401s in the failing sessions",
      "kill_if": "Failures occur within seconds of page load"
    }
  ],
  "first_action": "Join payment-gateway records to frontend session logs for the failing window and classify each blank-screen session as charged/not-charged and rendered/not-rendered; that single split discriminates between hypotheses 1-2 and 3-4"
}
```

Note the ranking logic: "payment sometimes goes through" is the most informative clue in the report, so hypotheses where the backend succeeds and the *display* fails outrank hypotheses where the request itself dies. Every hypothesis carries its own executioner — the evidence that would kill it — because a triage list you cannot prune is just a worry list.

```json
{
  "name": "split_bill",
  "purpose": "Split a bill evenly among a group",
  "inputs": {
    "total": {"type": "number", "meaning": "Full amount to split, in the bill's currency", "constraints": ">= 0"},
    "people": {"type": "integer", "meaning": "How many people share the bill", "constraints": ">= 1"}
  },
  "output": {"type": "number", "meaning": "Amount each person pays (total / people)"},
  "errors": [
    {"condition": "people == 0", "behaviour": "Raises an error instead of returning a value — division by zero is refused, not hidden"}
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Splitting is even; the contract says nothing about rounding, so 10.00 split 3 ways returns 3.333... and any rounding policy belongs to the caller until the contract says otherwise"
  ]
}
```

Writing a contract this way forces the two questions function documentation usually dodges: what exactly happens at the boundaries (zero people is specified, not discovered), and what is deliberately *not* promised (the rounding note). A caller reading this knows everything they depend on — and the author knows exactly what they may change later without breaking anyone: the internals, never the contract.

```json
{
  "incident": "Checkout errors caused by payment provider timeouts",
  "timeline": [
    {"time": "09:02", "event": "Detection", "detail": "Monitoring flagged high error rates on checkout"},
    {"time": "09:10", "event": "Diagnosis", "detail": "On-call confirmed the payment provider was timing out", "elapsed_from_detection": "8 min"},
    {"time": "09:25", "event": "Mitigation", "detail": "Checkout switched to the backup payment provider", "elapsed_from_detection": "23 min"},
    {"time": "09:31", "event": "Resolution", "detail": "Error rates returned to normal", "elapsed_from_detection": "29 min"},
    {"time": "next day", "event": "Follow-up", "detail": "Postmortem scheduled"}
  ],
  "summary": {"time_to_diagnose": "8 min", "time_to_mitigate": "23 min", "total_duration": "29 min"}
}
```

Structuring the narrative does two things prose hides. First, it separates detection from diagnosis — the 8-minute gap between 'something is wrong' and 'we know what' is where monitoring earns its money, and it is a different improvement project from the 15-minute mitigation gap that followed. Second, the elapsed fields make the incident comparable to the next one: a team tracking time-to-diagnose across incidents can tell whether its observability is improving, which a folder of prose timelines never answers. Keep timelines factual and blameless — events and times, not culprits; the postmortem is where causes get discussed.

